Translated Content:
When Aluda heard this, he readied the flint for his rifle;
He girded on his weapons, he sharpened his sword;
He inspected the hilt, lest the handle of the sword should break.

II. The sun rose high in the sky, mists descended into the ravines;
The giants of the Caucasus lean against the edge of the heavens.
Hawks, the slayers of birds, have set out for the hunt;
Kites follow the eagles, scavengers seeking a free meal.

He took the guest's armor, they invited him inside;
The woman followed behind, Jokola walked ahead;
Zviadauri followed along, having found a sworn brother.

IV. The man who was seen in the house, grey-streaked and aged,
He too rose to his feet, like a powerful mountain tiger.
A stranger come as a guest, he honors another’s guest;
To stand is the custom, and he cannot break the law of the mountains.
But when he saw the stranger, he took on the color of a wolf;
It was easy to discern what he was thinking.
The elderly Kist did not relish the sight of the foreign youth.
His heart beats with surging rage, and his face does not hide it;
His hand moves toward his dagger, quietly testing the blade,
But a guest in another’s house cannot unleash trouble.
He rose and went out silently, biting his finger bitterly;
Three times he struck his hand to his heart as he stepped outside.

I returned from the graveyard, searching for your horse in the ravines,
Instead of the horse, wretched me, I stumbled into the midst of devis.
One met me, clad in a black nabadi, of a colossal frame:
The stench of his skin still flashes before my eyes;
Large-eared, teeth-gnashing, hideous, black in color;
He reached out his hands to me, hands as large as a head,
A blackish leather hat on his head seemed like a small mountain.
He said to me: "Come with me, live with me, woman,
I have much gold and silver, I shall hide nothing from you."
I fled, terrified, and the devi pursued me with a roar;
The whole earth shook with a rumble beneath his feet;
That sound chilled me, as mountains and valleys spun topsy-turvy,
I barely reached here to tell you of my woe with a rasping cry.

IX. The next morning Aghaza drove the cow toward the mountain;
Birds were descending, drawn toward the dead;
Aghaza too steals away toward the cliff above the graveyard.
From there she scares off the ravens, the vultures with fluttering wings,
The insatiable eagles, screeching over the corpse;
She keeps her hands busy and hides her eyes, shining like the sun.
From the cliff she tosses pebbles while pretending to knit a sock,
Acting with skill, so that people cannot guess her thoughts.

They were met by the Kist army, hidden along the edges of the ravines;
They opened fire with their rifles, those hoop-bound barrels;
They yield no path to the Khevsur youths, who have sprung into action.
From both sides, they fire their guns amidst fierce war cries;
Both are burdened by their cause, and both fight with valor.
The bullets drank blood in abundance—grazing the mountain grass—
Yet the Kists did not break; they stand like a fortress tower.
Baburauli urged the Khevsurs to descend for sword-to-sword combat.
The battle came to the blade; the shield made way for the hewing;
The Khevsurs have a prize to claim—precious treasure and spoils.
O shield, do not fail the Gorda sword, for you are its companion;
As the Khevsurs rushed downward, a rocky ledge rose to meet them.

The wind beats, howling down from the mountains through the ravines;
A woman stands on a cliff, gazing at the river, her hair unbound;
She reminds me of a star whose light has faded, with pale, trembling lips.
She utters no sound… startled, she shudders as she looks down at the river;
How dreadfully it roars, how dreadfully it flows!
The snarling, cruel black valley rages;
If only someone would urge her: "Do not kill yourself!"
The woman closed her eyes and swiftly plunged into the whirlpool.

I. The Khevsurians were at the guest-house, beer was brewing in the vessel;
They sat upon the flat roof and feasted, drinking it from bowls;
Some played the panduri, singing loudly to its tune,
Laying heroic tales as a bridge to the listeners' souls;
With fervor, they sacredly recounted the names of heroes in verse;
The elders smoked their pipes, the smoke enveloping them like mist,
They interpreted their deeds and offered blessings to the heroes' spirits.
To instruct the youth, they recalled them with praise:
"It is up to you now, whoever among you shall prove to be a man!"

He is swift as a bullet, mystical as a serpent,
All of Pshav-Khevsureti worships him and holds him as their solace,
The crowned Queen Tamar always says this:
“As long as I have Mindia with me, and with him the mountain folk,
No matter how hard the enemy tries, they can never harm me.”
He knows the tactics to defeat and crush the enemy;
If he reaches the wounded while they still live, they need not fear death.
His peerless medicine can heal even one cut in two;
Because of this, his ranks never thin in war, and his army is always vast;
The story of his adventures has traveled far and wide.
